[Line "Euwe Variation: 5...Bd7 6.Be2 Nge7 7.O-O Ng6 8.g3 Be7 9.h4"]
[Section "5...Bd7: the Euwe Variation"]
[Summary "Black develops the bad bishop first and keeps the queen flexible. White plays the classical Be2, castles, and goes after the knight on g6 with g3 and h4."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 { [%cal Rd8b6,Rd7b5,Rc6b4] The Euwe Variation. Black plays the move he
will almost always want anyway, ...Bd7, and keeps the queen free for ...Qc7 or ...Qb6. The bishop can also head
for b5 or a4 to leave the chain. It is the main alternative to 5...Qb6 (Wikipedia, Werle, Schneider). }
6. Be2 { [%cal Ge1g1,Gb1a3] The classical move of Werle and Schneider, and of the 73-likes 'French Defense Repertoire' study. Werle's chapter covers 6...Nge7, 6...f6 and 6...Bb5; only the moves of the first two could be read. White
castles and decides between the Na3-c2 regroup and kingside play. }
( 6. Bb5? { A basic trap of the Euwe Variation: Black simply takes the pawn on e5. } 6... Nxe5! { If White recaptures with
the pawn, ...Bxb5 wins the bishop; if he takes on d7, the knight recaptures and Black is out of danger (the study
'French as White'). } 7. dxe5 Bxb5 )
( 6. a3?! { A wasted move here: the queen is not on b6, so a3 hits nothing and Black uses the tempo to attack
e5 (Wikipedia: Black at least equalises after 6...f6). } 6... f6! { The break comes at once, and White's centre is
under pressure before the kingside is developed. } 7. Bd3 Qc7 8. O-O O-O-O 9. Re1 c4 10. Bc2 Nge7 { This is the
line Wikibooks gives; Black has the e5-pawn in his sights and a king on the queenside. } )
6... Nge7 { The main move: the knight goes to f5 or g6, and the f8-bishop keeps its diagonal. } 7. O-O { [%cal Gf1e1]
White castles. }
( 7. Na3 { The quiet alternative, and the main line in Wikibooks. **Idea:** the knight goes round to c2, where it
defends d4. } 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 9. Nc2 Qb6 10. O-O Rc8 )
( 7. Na3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 9. g4 { A sharp try from the 73-likes 'French Defense Repertoire' study (Black's side): the pawn kicks the knight on f5. } 9... Nh4 10. Nxh4
Qxh4 11. Bf4 )
7... Ng6 { [%cal Gg6h4,Rg6e5] The knight attacks e5 and heads for h4. } 8. g3 { [%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5] **Idea:** the pawn takes h4
away from the knight and prepares h4-h5 to hit the one on g6. } 8... Be7 9. h4 { [%cal Gh4h5,Rg6h4] **Plan:** h5 drives
the knight away, usually to e7 or h8, and wins time and space on the kingside. Schneider's course gives this line
as the main one; the confirmed sources stop here, so this is a draft. Watch for ...f6 once the knight has
left g6. } *

[Line "5...Bd7 6.Be2 Nge7 7.O-O: 7...Nf5 and 7...cxd4"]
[Section "5...Bd7: the Euwe Variation"]
[Summary "Black heads for f5 at once or releases the tension first. White uses Na3-c2 or Nc3 and meets ...Nf5 with Bd3 and the trade on f5."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Be2 Nge7 7. O-O Nf5 { [%cal Gf5d4] The knight attacks d4 straight away. }
( 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 9. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3b5] } 9... Rc8 10. Bg5 { [%cal Gg5d8] } 10... Qb6 11. Na4 Qa5 12. Rc1 h6 13. Be3 Be7
14. Bd3 O-O { The Dannyburger study (113 likes) from which this comes calls it a pretty equal position with a solid
structure. } )
8. Na3 { [%cal Ga3c2] The knight heads for c2 to defend d4. } 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 Be7 10. Nc2 O-O 11. Bd3 { [%cal Gd3f5] The bishop
now offers the trade on f5, which removes Black's best minor piece. } 11... f6 { Played only now that Black has
castled (the study's advice for Black). } 12. Bf4 { [%cal Gf4e5] The e5-pawn is overprotected rather than given up, as
Chess in the Library recommends against ...f6. } 12... Rc8 13. Bxf5 exf5 14. Re1 Be8 15. exf6 Bxf6 { **Verdict:** a quiet
equal position (the Complete Repertoire study, 777 likes, written from Black's side, ends here). White gets little
against 7...Nf5 in this line; the sharper tries are the 7.Na3 lines above. } *

[Line "5...Bd7 6.Be2 f6"]
[Section "5...Bd7: the Euwe Variation"]
[Summary "The break ...f6 as early as move six. White takes on e5 and the structure becomes double-edged: Black has the centre pawns and the king on the queenside, White the initiative."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Be2 f6 { [%cal Rf6e5] Black attacks e5 immediately. He usually takes on
e5 and plays ...Qc7 and ...O-O-O, with a very double-edged game (Wikibooks). } 7. O-O fxe5 8. Nxe5 { [%cal Ge5d7,Ge5c6]
Wikibooks recaptures with the knight. }
( 8. dxe5 { The Complete Repertoire study's line, from Black's side. } 8... Qc7 9. Bf4 Nh6 10. c4 d4 11. Nbd2 Be7
12. Ne1 O-O-O )
8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Qc7 10. Re1 O-O-O { The source stops here, so this is a draft. The structure is double-edged, as Wikibooks says: Black
has the centre pawns and the king on the queenside, White the pawn on e5 and the open f-file. } *

[Line "5...Bd7 6.Bd3: the sharp alternative"]
[Section "5...Bd7: the Euwe Variation"]
[Summary "Against the Euwe Variation the gambit bishop move still works: 6...Qb6 transposes to the Milner-Barry, and 6...Rc8 needs a precise 7.a3."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Bd3 { [%cal Gd3b5,Rc6b4] The sharp choice (Chess in the Library, the
Lichess study 'French as White'). If Black plays ...Qb6 the game transposes to the Milner-Barry; if he plays
...cxd4 the gambit follows. The Euwe player's own idea is the one to know: ...Rc8, ...Nb4 and ...cxd4, with a knight
jump to c2. }
6... Rc8 { [%cal Gc6b4,Rc5d4,Rb4c2] Black prepares ...Nb4 attacking the bishop, and ...cxd4. If White castles now, the
knight takes over c2. }
7. a3! { [%cal Ga3b4] Takes b4 away from the knight and the bishop, and most of Black's ideas stop working. The game is
even and White can develop and go for a kingside attack. }
( 7. O-O? { The move Black hopes for. } 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nb4 { [%cal Gb4c2,Gb4d3] The knight attacks the bishop and
jumps to c2; the study says Black usually wins if he is prepared. } )
( 7. Na3 { Surprisingly good (the study): the bishop can tuck itself into b1 and the knight goes to c2, where it
is safe from tactics and adds a defender to d4. } )
7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Qb6 { Black adds a third attacker to d4. } 9. O-O { **Plan:** reach a version of the Milner-Barry. The
study calls this a bit more dubious than the real gambit but statistically quite good. The 'correct' move is to
add a defender with Bc2, but then ...g5 is a sharp idea for Black that White has to meet with precision. }
9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 11. Nc3 Qxe5 12. Re1 { **Verdict:** the same pin on the e6-pawn as in the Milner-Barry
file; the study rates this a little worse than the main gambit, but White's practical chances are good. } *
